    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch02.s10.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s10.p01.01",
            "text": "Why are token embeddings needed after tokenization has already turned text into token IDs?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s10.p01.02",
            "text": "Token embeddings are also called vector embeddings, and they are sometimes called word embeddings, but \"word embeddings\" is not entirely accurate because tokens can be words, subwords, or even characters.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@25",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@28",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@30",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@32",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@36",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@41",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@44",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@47"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s10.p01.03",
            "text": "A word-based tokenizer makes each token one word, but GPT uses byte pair encoding as a subword tokenizer, so even parts of words or characters can be individual tokens.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@80",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@82",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@86",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@89",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@92",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@96",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@98",
              "mk-6cFebjis@159",
              "mk-6cFebjis@161"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s10.p01.04",
            "text": "Computers require numerical representations of words, because they cannot understand words directly as inputs to a machine learning model or a large language model.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@261",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@263",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@266",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@268",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@270",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@273",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@275",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3882",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3885",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3887"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s10.p01.05",
            "text": "Token embeddings are the step that converts token IDs into vectors.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@163",
              "mk-6cFebjis@165"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s10.p01.06",
            "text": "They are needed because token IDs alone are not sufficient as model inputs.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@306",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@308",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@310",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@319",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@322",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@326",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3858",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3860",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3863",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3866",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3869",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3870",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3873",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3875"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s10.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s10.p02.01",
            "text": "The problem is not numbers by themselves, but what those numbers fail to represent.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s10.p02.02",
            "text": "Assigning random numbers or token IDs to words does not capture semantic relationships between related words such as \"cat\" and \"kitten\".",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@329",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@331",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@334",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@337",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@339",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@342",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@346",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@348",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@351",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@353",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@356",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@360",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@363",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@366",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3913",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3915",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3917",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3919",
              "mk-6cFebjis@3921",
